It is 9:40 on a Tuesday night in Peenya 2nd Stage, Bangalore. Ramesh is still at his desk, signing a ₹14,000 purchase order for welding rods. His phone buzzes. The purchase head at his biggest customer, a pump maker, wants 5% off next quarter's rates. Ramesh has run Sri Lakshmi Precision Fabricators for fourteen years. He works 75 hours a week, and revenue has sat at ₹18 crore for three years.

Ramesh is fictional, a composite of owners I meet across Bangalore's industrial belts, but his week is real enough. When owners like him look for a **business coach for MSME and small business owners**, they usually want help selling more. That is the wrong place to start. A stalled small manufacturer rarely has a sales problem first. It has an order-of-work problem: the owner chases revenue before fixing pricing, and hires people before building systems, so every fix makes him busier. The four fixes below work only in sequence.

---

## What a business coach for MSME owners actually does

Three words get used loosely here.

- A **consultant** studies your business and gives you answers.
- A **mentor** has walked a similar road and tells you what they learned.
- A **business coach** does something narrower, and for a stuck owner, more useful.

The coach agrees with you on the order in which things get fixed, then holds you to it every week, including the weeks you would rather skip. At [Simpleworks Consulting](https://simpleworks.in/about), most of my work with founders blends the three, but the weekly holding-to-account is what owners say they could not do alone.

Ramesh's numbers show why order matters. His father set up the unit in 1989. Today it has 45 people, laser cutting, three press brakes and a welding bay, with powder coating sent out. Three customers, the pump maker and two equipment makers, bring in 60% of revenue. Jobs are priced the way his father priced them: a rate per kilo of finished steel, plus a markup.

Peenya makes that habit expensive. The Karnataka State Pollution Control Board describes the Peenya industrial area as one of the oldest and largest in South-East Asia, spread over about 40 square kilometres. Thousands of units buy steel at the same prices and chase the same customers. When everyone's material cost is the same, the price you quote decides whether you make money.

Before he found a coach, Ramesh tried three fixes. He hired a sales executive, who brought in small enquiries that took as long to quote as big ones, and who left after eight months. He bought an ERP licence that now produces invoices and nothing else. And he worked longer. Each fix made sense on its own. Together they piled more work on the one person who was already the bottleneck.

---

## Fix pricing before you try to grow revenue

The first month involved no selling at all. His coach asked for the last 40 jobs and costed each one properly: steel at the actual purchase price, laser and press-brake hours, setup time, consumables, rework, power, and the cost of waiting to be paid.

Ramesh had never counted that last one. The pump maker paid in 75 days. At a 10.5% interest rate on his working-capital limit, that is a little over 2% of every invoice gone before the money arrives.

The result was uncomfortable. Thirteen of the 40 jobs lost money. Nearly all were short runs of thin sheet with many bends, mostly the pump maker's enclosures. Setting up the press brake took longer than running the batch. The per-kilo rate hid this, because thin sheet weighs little and a bend costs the same whatever the steel weighs. Charging by the kilo for bent sheet is like a tailor charging by the metre of cloth when the real cost is in the stitching.

Why does pricing come first? In "Managing Price, Gaining Profit" (*Harvard Business Review*, 1992), Michael Marn and Robert Rosiello of McKinsey studied 2,463 companies. On average, a 1% improvement in price lifted operating profit by 11.1%. A 1% rise in volume lifted it by 3.3%. And volume at a bad price multiplies the loss. Had Ramesh sold harder first, he would have filled his shop with more of the jobs that were costing him money.

So he changed how he quotes. Every quote now shows material, machine hours, a setup charge and a line for the credit period. There is a minimum order value of ₹25,000 per drawing. The job types that were losing money went up by 8% to 14%.

---

## Grow revenue from the customers you can price well

The pump maker said no. Over four months it moved about half its enclosure work to a cheaper fabricator in Bommasandra. Ramesh's revenue dropped for two quarters, and he admits he nearly reversed the whole exercise in month five.

This is where most owners abandon pricing work, and where a coach earns the fee. In the Monday review, the question moved away from how to win the work back. It became: what do we do with the press-brake hours that just came free?

The answer was rooftop solar. Installers across Bangalore need galvanised mounting structures, delivered fast, in medium runs with simple bends. That is work Ramesh's shop does well and can price well. Demand has grown since the central government launched the PM Surya Ghar rooftop scheme in 2024. His production supervisor built sample structures for three solar contractors in the north of the city. Two became regular customers within six months.

To grow revenue properly, Ramesh also had to get sales off his own desk. Quoting moved to the production planner, who now works from the new costing sheet. Ramesh meets only the top five accounts, once a month. By the end of the year, the three biggest customers made up about 45% of revenue, down from 60%. That matters as much as the new sales. A customer who brings in a third of your revenue sets your price, whether you notice it or not.

---

## Build systems so the shop floor stops waiting for you

Better prices and new customers created more decisions, and every one of them still came to Ramesh. His coach asked him to log each decision brought to him for 30 days: what it was, who brought it, and how long it waited.

There were 212 in the month. About three-quarters were routine: purchases under ₹50,000, overtime approvals, dispatch order, small rework calls. Each one waited for Ramesh, sometimes for two days, while the person who asked stood idle.

Ramesh built three things to fix it, and each fits on a single page.

- **Approval limits by name.** The purchase manager signs orders up to ₹50,000. The plant head decides overtime and dispatch priority. Accounts releases payments to approved vendors on fixed days.
- **Three one-page procedures:** how a quote is built, how a purchase is raised and how a dispatch is cleared. Each is a checklist, not a manual.
- **A 45-minute review every Monday at nine,** on five numbers: order book, on-time dispatch, rework, receivables over 60 days and cash in the bank.

Checklists sound too simple to matter. The evidence says otherwise. In 2009, the *New England Journal of Medicine* published a study led by Atul Gawande on the WHO Surgical Safety Checklist across eight hospitals. Major complications fell from 11% to 7%, and inpatient deaths from 1.5% to 0.8%. The surgeons did not get more skilled. The checklist caught what skilled people skip under pressure. A shop floor works the same way. Ramesh's people know the work. The checklist means nobody has to ask him which step comes next.

One side effect: the ERP finally became useful, because the procedures now say what goes into it and when.

---

## A team that runs without you takes a year, not a hire

Systems make delegation possible. They do not make it happen. Ramesh promoted Manjunath, his production supervisor of eleven years, to plant head. He hired a costing and accounts executive, the first person in the company whose job was to know the numbers. And he gave Farooq, his purchase manager, the ₹50,000 limit.

In month seven, Farooq ordered ₹38,000 of the wrong grade of galvanised sheet for a solar job. Ramesh found out on a Thursday, and every instinct told him to take purchase approvals back. He didn't. In Monday's review, the team traced the mistake to a drawing that did not state the coating thickness, and added one line to the quote checklist. Farooq has not repeated the error. More important, he did not start bringing every order back to Ramesh's desk.

That is how most owners undo delegation. They overrule the first mistake, and the team learns the limit was never real. I have written more about moving decision rights in [how to make your business run without the owner](https://www.simpleworks.in/blog/how-to-make-your-business-run-without-the-owner). The point here is time. You build a team that runs without you over a year of not overruling people. One senior hire will not do it.

In month ten, Ramesh spent a Wednesday afternoon at his daughter's school annual day. His phone stayed in his pocket. Nothing waited for him.

---

## "Can't I just do this myself?"

This is the strongest objection, and it deserves a straight answer. Ramesh has spent close to thirty years around sheet metal. No coach knows more about bending steel than he does. Much of what is sold as business coaching in India is motivation and borrowed frameworks at a premium price. Nothing in this story is secret: a costing sheet, a decision log, a Monday review. An owner could read this post and do all of it himself. For a unit under about ₹3 crore in revenue, a good chartered accountant and some discipline may be all you need.

Most of that is true. A good business coach for MSME and small business owners brings almost nothing about the trade. What the coach brings is two things an owner cannot give himself.

The first is an outside view. Ramesh looked at per-kilo pricing for fourteen years and never questioned it, because it was his father's system and it had worked.

The second is accountability in month five, when revenue is falling and every instinct says reverse. Self-coaching tends to fail at exactly the point where the plan gets uncomfortable, which is usually the point where it starts to work.

There is a real limit too. Coaching fails if the owner will not let go. If Ramesh had overruled Farooq, no coach could have rescued the year. When you choose a coach, pick someone who has run operations in a business like yours. Then judge them by one test: six months in, does your Monday review still happen without them chasing you?

---

## What Ramesh actually paid for

Twelve months on, revenue was ₹19.4 crore, barely up. Operating margin rose from about 6% to about 10.5%, which means operating profit nearly doubled. The order book for the coming year points to about ₹23 crore. Ramesh works around 55 hours a week instead of 75.

Advice was never what he lacked. He had plenty of it from his CA, his customers and his WhatsApp groups. He bought a sequence, and someone to hold him to it when it hurt. That is the honest case for hiring a business coach for MSME and small business owners: the order is the product.

So which fix is your first?

- If you cannot say which of your last 20 jobs made money, start with pricing.
- If three customers bring in more than half your revenue, work on revenue next.
- If more than ten decisions a day wait for you, build systems.
- If you cannot take a week off without the phone ringing, build the team, once the systems exist.

Start with the one you have been avoiding. In my experience, it is usually pricing.

